      ],
      "forms": [
        "hin",
        "hín"
      ],
      "gloss": "snow; Snow",
      "difficulty": "difficult",
      "evidence": "The main sníh entry compares Shina hin snow, but numbered addenda 14840 and 14845 offer *himna instead, with an explicit preference question. The modern forms alone cannot decide between these competing histories; retain both and seek the later discussion before assigning an exact parent.",
      "sourceEntries": [
        "13798",
        "14840",
        "14845"
      ],
      "primaryEvidenceText": {
        "13798": "13798 sníh f. 'wetness, moisture' TS. (snḗhiti-, snī́hiti- f. pl. 'waters' RV., snĭ̄hiti- f. 'moisture' TĀr., snīhán- m. or snīhā́- f. 'mucus of nose' ŚBr.), '*snow' R. L. Turner BSOAS xviii 447, xix 375. [√snih] Pk. siṇhā- f. 'snow, dew, mist, drop of water falling from sky' (metath. of *snihā-); Sh.gil. hĭn m. 'snow', gur. koh. hinn f., jij. hī˜n (< *hisnā, cf. metath. in Pa. suṇhā- < snuṣā-), Sv. hina (NTS v 340 -n- after hēyán 'winter'); — altern. < śīná-¹ X himá-: Tor. hīn m. 'snow, ice', Mai. (Barth) \"hīṇ\" 'snow' NTS xviii 124; — or < svinná- (but with unexpl. -h-): L. sinnhā m. 'unseasoned wood', (Ju.) sinnā̃ 'moist, wet'; P. sinnhā 'wet', sinnhṇā 'to be wet'. — See Add. *snihācala-. Addenda: sníh-: WPah.kṭg. sínnhɔ 'wet'.",
        "14840": "14840 sníh-: Sh. hĭn &c. 'snow', Sv. hina altern. < *himna- ? *sparvati: see also phalph- Add.",
        "14845": "14845 *himna-: Sh.gil. hĭn m., gur. koh. hinn f., jij. hĭ̄n 'snow', Sv. hina rather than < sníh- ?"
      },
      "assignments": []
    },
    {
      "formIds": [
        "f_rzs65fsp735wq",
        "f_l7oxncmf4ageu",
        "f_oypicyx7if7zc",
        "f_vlx5763xele62",
        "f_q5wim3covok5y"
      ],
      "forms": [
        "síŋil",
        "síŋel"
      ],
      "gloss": "sand; Sand",
      "difficulty": "difficult",
      "evidence": "Turner’s sikatā entry explicitly discusses Iranian influence on s-initial forms and possible borrowing or suffix variation in the g/k and l/t series. Degener’s T. 13386 citation for sigal supports family identification, but the modern siŋel/siŋil forms add a nasal absent from the cited Shina sigal. Neither the immediate donor nor this nasal development is established; do not flatten the comparison into an unqualified inherited reflex.",
      "sourceEntries": [
        "13386"
      ],
      "primaryEvidenceText": {
        "13386": "13386 síkatā f. 'grain of sand', (usu. pl.) 'sand, gravel' VS., sigatā- f. Kap. [Collective formation from sika- in śvēta-sika-druka- 'scattering white sand' TĀr. 1. 12. 3 A. Sharma in Bhārata-kaumudī 873. Kaf. and some Dard. forms attest IA. ś- corresponding to Ir. θ- and s- (OPers. θikā-, Bal. six, Psht. šəga, &c. EVP 73) and support E. Benveniste BSL xxx 60 in assumption that s- forms of Sk. and other Dard. languages and WPah. (which distinguish ś and s) are loans from or influenced by Iranian. Variations in Kaf. and Dard. of -g- ~ -k- and -l- ~ -t- may be due to borrowings within that group or to later Ir. influences or they may, in part at least, be due to suffix. variation like that of síkatā- ~ *sikatī- (in sikatyà- VS.) ~ siktā- lex. ~ *sikyā-] *śikatā-: Kt. ċü, ċüyü 'sand', Wg. šō, Ash. šō̃ṛə, Dm. ċī˜a, Kho. šuγúr (< earlier *šiguδ → Kal.rumb. šīgou, st. šīgol-, urt. šigər). síkatā: Pa. sikatā- f. 'sand, gravel', NiDoc. siǵataṁmi loc. sg.; Pk. sikayā-, sigayā- f. 'sand', Paš.dar. sē̆o, chil. sīóu, kuṛ. sīu, ar. siō, Shum. sī́u, Gaw. sī́ũ; — ← Ir. dialect preserving -t- (?): Woṭ. sigit f. (šigit infl. by Psht. šəga Buddruss Woṭ 126), Bshk. sigit f. *sikyā-, *sikyatā- (cf. Ir. *sikya- E. Benveniste loc. cit.): K. sĕkh, dat. sĕki f. 'sand, file' (semant. cf. rētra-); WPah.khaś. sikk 'gravel', bhal. sikkā f. Ext. -l- (cf. sikatilá- 'sandy' Bhartr̥.) perh. in Kt. ċele 'sand', Paš.lauṛ. siyēl m., gul. sēl m. IIFL iii 3, 161, Tor. sigλ'l m., Chil. Gau. sigil, Sv. šigala (š- from Psht. šəga → Phal. šíga f. NOPhal 48); Phal. sígλl m. 'fine sand'; Sh.gil. sĭgăl m. 'sand', pales. sλgλl. sikatilá- see síkatā-. Addenda: síkatā-: x śarkarā-¹ (Pk. sikkarā-) Add²; poss. also in Pk. sikayā-, K. sēk-, WPah. sikk, sikkā."
      },
